        "content": "from agent.graph import should_auto_apply, AUTO_APPLY_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD, AUTO_APPLY_MAX_SEVERITY\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"confidence,severity,expected\", [\n    (0.85, 2, True),                                   # exact threshold, boundary severity\n    (0.849999, 2, False),                               # just under confidence\n    (0.85, 3, False),                                   # confidence ok, severity too high\n    (1.0, 4, False),                                    # max confidence can't override severity\n    (0.0, 1, False),\n])\ndef test_auto_apply_gate_boundaries(confidence, severity, expected):\n    assert should_auto_apply(confidence=confidence, severity=severity) is expected\n\ndef test_constants_match_agents_md():\n    assert AUTO_APPLY_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD == 0.85\n    assert AUTO_APPLY_MAX_SEVERITY == 2\n```\n\n**`engine/pipeline/detect.py::severity_for_orphan`** — trivial today but load-bearing.\n\n```python\ndef test_orphan_severity_with_children_is_4():\n    assert severity_for_orphan(has_children=True) == 4\n\ndef test_orphan_severity_without_children_is_3():\n    assert severity_for_orphan(has_children=False) == 3\n```\n\n**`web/lib/reviewer-label.ts::reviewerLabel`** — precedence logic (username > email > fallback) is exactly the kind of thing that silently breaks.\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/reviewer-label.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { reviewerLabel } from \"./reviewer-label\";\n\ndescribe(\"reviewerLabel\", () => {\n  it(\"prefers username over email\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: \"j.rao\", primaryEmailAddress: { emailAddress: \"j@x.com\" } }, \"uid_1\"))\n      .toBe(\"j.rao\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back to email when username is null\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: null, primaryEmailAddress: { emailAddress: \"j@x.com\" } }, \"uid_1\"))\n      .toBe(\"j@x.com\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back to the stable id when user is null\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel(null, \"uid_1\")).toBe(\"uid_1\");\n  });\n  it(\"falls back to the stable id when username and email are both absent\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: null, primaryEmailAddress: null }, \"uid_1\")).toBe(\"uid_1\");\n  });\n  it(\"treats empty-string username as absent, not as a valid label\", () => {\n    // EDGE CASE — current `??` chain does NOT catch this; \"\" is truthy-absent\n    // only for null/undefined. Verify intended behavior before trusting output.\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: \"\", primaryEmailAddress: { emailAddress: \"j@x.com\" } }, \"uid_1\"))\n      .toBe(\"\"); // <- likely a bug: renders blank instead of falling through\n  });\n});\n```\n\nThat last case is a **real edge-case gap**: `user?.username ?? ...` doesn't guard against an empty-string username, which Clerk could plausibly return for a not-yet-set profile — worth fixing or explicitly asserting as intended.\n\n**`web/types/rules.ts::ruleSpecificity`** and **`web/types/source.ts` schemas** — the TS mirror of the Python contract above; must be tested in parallel since AGENTS.md requires both sides to match on every change.\n\n```ts\n// web/types/rules.test.ts\nimport { survivorshipRuleSchema, ruleSpecificity } from \"./rules\";\n\nit(\"rejects prefer_source without preferredSourceId\", () => {\n  const result = survivorshipRuleSchema.safeParse({\n    id: \"1\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"prefer_source\", description: \"\",\n  });\n  expect(result.success).toBe(false);\n});\n\nit(\"mirrors engine ordering: exact field+type beats wildcard-either\", () => {\n  const exact = { id: \"1\", field: \"territory\", entityType: \"account\", strategy: \"most_recent\", description: \"\" };\n  const wild = { id: \"2\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"most_recent\", description: \"\" };\n  expect(ruleSpecificity(exact as any)).toBeGreaterThan(ruleSpecificity(wild as any));\n});\n```\n\n```ts\n// web/types/source.test.ts\nimport { sourceConfigSchema, createSourceSchema } from \"./source\";\n\nit(\"rejects an empty objects array\", () => {\n  expect(sourceConfigSchema.safeParse({ objects: [] }).success).toBe(false);\n});\nit(\"rejects a source name over 80 chars\", () => {\n  const bad = { name: \"a\".repeat(81), kind: \"salesforce\", config: { objects: [\"Account\"] } };\n  expect(createSourceSchema.safeParse(bad).success).toBe(false);\n});\nit(\"never accepts a credential-shaped key in config (regression guard)\", () => {\n  // AGENTS.md: sources.config must never hold secrets.\n  const withSecret = { objects: [\"Account\"], password: \"hunter2\" } as any;\n  const parsed = sourceConfigSchema.parse({ objects: [\"Account\"] });\n  expect(parsed).not.toHaveProperty(\"password\"); // schema strips unknown keys by default — confirm, don't assume\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "3. Component logic gaps — `SeverityMark`",
        "content": "\nEncodes a hard design constraint (\"never render the mark without the word,\" AGENTS.md § grayscale). No test enforces this contract, so a future refactor could silently drop the label.\n\n```tsx\n// web/components/severity-mark.test.tsx\nimport { render, screen } from \"@testing-library/react\";\nimport { SeverityMark } from \"./severity-mark\";\n\nit.each([1, 2, 3, 4] as const)(\"severity %i always renders both mark and label\", (sev) => {\n  render(<SeverityMark severity={sev} />);\n  expect(screen.getByText(/structural|attribute|cosmetic/i)).toBeInTheDocument();\n});\n\nit(\"renders solid fill for severity 4 and no border for severity 1 (density, not hue)\", () => {\n  const { container: c4 } = render(<SeverityMark severity={4} />);\n  const { container: c1 } = render(<SeverityMark severity={1} />);\n  expect(c4.querySelector(\"span > span\")?.className).toMatch(/bg-ink/);\n  expect(c1.querySelector(\"span > span\")?.className).not.toMatch(/border/);\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "4. Unimplemented functions — TDD skeletons (Phase 2/3, write now against the documented contract)",
        "content": "\nSince AGENTS.md mandates \"smallest useful version first\" and pure-function pipeline stages specifically so they're testable, these skeletons should be written **before** the implementation, marked `xfail`, and un-marked as each lands:\n\n```python\n",
